--TEST--
A shape literal returned from an arrow body and one discarded as a statement are both written parenthesized — `rule:types/anonymous-object`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:types/anonymous-object`: the parser commits a statement-initial `{` to a block, and a
// `{` straight after `=>` to a block body, so both positions spell a shape
// literal `({...})`. That is the same fix JavaScript uses for the same
// ambiguity, and it is the whole of it — nothing else about the literal
// changes.

// Out of an arrow body, observed through a callback a `Core` member invokes.
array<int> $ns = [1, 2];
var $shapes = Core\Arr::map($ns, fn(int $n): {n: int} => ({n: $n * 10}));
foreach ($shapes as {n: int} $s) {
    echo $s->n, "\n";
}

// Discarded as a statement: the literal is built and the frame that built it
// releases it — a no-op with an allocation in it. It lowers rather than
// panicking because § 2 names the spelling, and the field it captured is
// still the caller's afterwards.
({c: 3});
string $held = "kept";
({label: $held});
echo $held, "\n";
--EXPECT--
10
20
kept
